    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! For cooling down cooked poultry to control the growth of Clostridium perfringens, the safe temperature range is 70°F (21°C) to 125°F (51°C) within the first two hours. After that, the temperature should drop below 40°F (4°C) within the next four hours. This rapid cooling process - first down to 70°F/21°C and then to below 40°F/4°C as quickly as possible - reduces the risk of bacterial growth and ensures food safety. Ensure your cold holding equipment and cooling process are calibrated to meet these requirements.",
